  "description": "Six-act difficulty ramp for a 50-maze candy-collecting book, ages 6 to 10. Loop and dead-end ranges are PRD 17.4's normative table verbatim. Grids grow 8x8 to 18x18; the 0.375 in cell floor at 18x18 is the practical minimum for a child drawing with a pencil. Dead-end count rises only gently, 4-5 to 9-10, so dead-end density *falls* with grid size, from about 7% of cells at 8x8 to about 3% at 18x18: later mazes get their difficulty from the loop budget and the sheer distance to the finish, not from a proliferation of wrong turns, and every wrong turn stays short because the depth ceiling stays under seven steps everywhere. collectibleScale and deadEndScale are 0.70 and 0.52, not the 0.55/0.50 pair they started as: a reviewer reading real printed pages could not tell a chocolate bar from a bat at the 16x16 size, and the tick boxes ask a child to make exactly that distinction. Both are monochrome silhouettes of similar weight, so size is the only signal left, and a 10% difference is not one. The ratio stops at 1.35 rather than going further because the finale's cell is 8.3 mm: shrinking scenery to 0.38 put it at 2.9 mm, where the press cannot hold any feature the drawing is made of, and an unreadable smudge is not a clearer signal than a small one.",
